<p>15. <strong>What is hoisting in JavaScript?</strong></p>
<p>Answer: Hoisting is JavaScript's default behavior of moving declarations to the top of the current scope (script or function). Only declarations are hoisted, not initializations. This means you can use a variable before it is declared, but it will be <code>undefined</code> until the assignment is executed.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">console.log(x); // undefined
